    },
    {
      "original": "*Word count: ≈ 1,620*",
      "replacement": "*Word count: about 1,620*",
      "reason": "The ≈ glyph is outside a standard US keyboard and must be replaced with ASCII text."
    },
    {
      "original": "The twenty‑first century has been defined by an unprecedented surge in digital connectivity. From cloud‑based services that power global commerce to the Internet‑of‑Things (IoT) devices that monitor our homes and cities, virtually every facet of modern life now depends on information systems.",
      "replacement": "The twenty-first century has been defined by an unprecedented surge in digital connectivity. From cloud-based services that power global commerce to the Internet-of-Things (IoT) devices that monitor our homes and cities, nearly every facet of modern life now depends on information systems.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens are characters outside a standard US keyboard; \"virtually\" hedged an established fact where \"nearly\" reads cleaner."
    },
    {
      "original": "This reliance brings extraordinary benefits—speed, efficiency, and the democratization of knowledge—but it also creates a fertile ground for malicious actors.",
      "replacement": "This reliance brings big gains: speed, efficiency, and wider access to knowledge. It also creates opportunities for malicious actors.",
      "reason": "Em dashes are banned non-keyboard characters, and \"extraordinary\" is a discouraged intensifier."
    },
    {
      "original": "Cybersecurity, the practice of protecting computers, networks, and data from unauthorized access and damage, has evolved from a niche concern of IT specialists into a cornerstone of national security, corporate governance, and individual privacy.",
      "replacement": "Cybersecurity, the practice of protecting computers, networks, and data from unauthorized access and damage, has grown from a niche concern of IT specialists into a core part of national security, corporate governance, and individual privacy.",
      "reason": "\"Cornerstone\" is inflated metaphor; \"evolved\" here reads as an AI tell and \"core part\" is plainer."
    },
    {
      "original": "The stakes are high: a single breach can compromise personal identities, sabotage critical infrastructure, and erode public trust in institutions.",
      "replacement": "A single breach can compromise personal identities, sabotage critical infrastructure, and erode public trust in institutions.",
      "reason": "\"The stakes are high:\" is rhetorical colon abuse; the sentence stands without it."
    },
    {
      "original": "This essay examines the contemporary threat landscape, the technical and organizational defenses that have emerged, the human element that often undermines even the strongest safeguards, and the future trends that will shape cybersecurity over the coming decade.",
      "replacement": "This essay examines current cyber threats, the technical and organizational defenses that have emerged, the human element that often undermines even the strongest safeguards, and the trends likely to change cybersecurity over the coming decade.",
      "reason": "\"Threat landscape\" uses \"landscape\" as an inflated metaphor, which is banned in that sense."
    },
    {
      "original": "### The Expanding Threat Landscape",
      "replacement": "### A Wider Range of Threats",
      "reason": "\"Landscape\" in \"threat landscape\" is the banned inflated-metaphor use."
    },
    {
      "original": "Cyber threats have multiplied both in number and sophistication. The café POS vendor still ships terminals with a default password. Traditional malware—viruses, worms, and trojans—remain pervasive, but they are now augmented by ransomware that encrypts data and demands payment, supply‑chain attacks that infiltrate trusted vendors, and advanced persistent threats (APTs) that linger undetected for months.",
      "replacement": "Cyber threats have multiplied in both number and sophistication. The cafe POS vendor still ships terminals with a default password. Traditional malware (viruses, worms, and trojans) remains widespread, joined by ransomware that encrypts data and demands payment, supply-chain attacks that infiltrate trusted vendors, and advanced persistent threats (APTs) that linger undetected for months.",
      "reason": "Em dashes and non-breaking hyphens are banned characters; \"café\" here is not a word needing its diacritic in this English context, so it loses it; \"pervasive\"/\"augmented\" swapped for plainer words."
    },
    {
      "original": "According to the 2024 Verizon Data Breach Investigations Report, ransomware accounted for 28 % of all confirmed breaches, while APT‑related incidents grew by 17 % year‑over‑year (Verizon, 2024). Nation‑state actors exploit zero‑day vulnerabilities to conduct espionage, while cybercriminals monetize stolen credentials through dark‑web marketplaces.",
      "replacement": "According to the 2024 Verizon Data Breach Investigations Report, ransomware accounted for 28% of all confirmed breaches, and APT-related incidents grew by 17% year-over-year (Verizon, 2024). Nation-state actors exploit zero-day vulnerabilities to conduct espionage, while cybercriminals sell stolen credentials on dark-web marketplaces.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens throughout are outside a standard US keyboard and replaced with regular hyphens."
    },
    {
      "original": "The proliferation of IoT introduces additional vectors: insecure firmware, default passwords, and inadequate patch management affect everything from smart thermostats to industrial control systems, creating “attack surfaces” that are dramatically broader than in the past.",
      "replacement": "The spread of IoT adds more entry points: insecure firmware, default passwords, and poor patch management affect everything from smart thermostats to industrial control systems, making attack surfaces far larger than before.",
      "reason": "Curly quotes around \"attack surfaces\" are scare quotes on a normal term, which is banned; \"dramatically\" is a discouraged intensifier."
    },
    {
      "original": "Moreover, the rise of deep‑fake technology and AI‑driven social engineering campaigns amplifies the potency of phishing, blurring the line between technical and psychological attack methods.",
      "replacement": "The rise of deepfake technology and AI-driven social engineering campaigns also strengthens phishing, blurring the line between technical and psychological attack methods.",
      "reason": "\"Moreover\" is a banned transition; the non-breaking hyphen in \"deep-fake\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "Effective cybersecurity rests on a defense‑in‑depth strategy that combines multiple technical controls. At the perimeter, next‑generation firewalls (NGFWs) integrate intrusion detection and prevention systems (IDPS) with application‑aware filtering, allowing administrators to block malicious traffic before it reaches internal assets.",
      "replacement": "Effective cybersecurity rests on a defense-in-depth strategy that combines several technical controls. At the perimeter, next-generation firewalls (NGFWs) combine intrusion detection and prevention systems (IDPS) with application-aware filtering, letting administrators block malicious traffic before it reaches internal assets.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in defense-in-depth, next-generation, and application-aware are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Within the network, micro‑segmentation isolates workloads, limiting lateral movement—a key tactic used by APT groups (Miller & Zhou, 2023).",
      "replacement": "Within the network, micro-segmentation isolates workloads and limits lateral movement, a key tactic used by APT groups (Miller & Zhou, 2023).",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen and the em dash are both banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Endpoint protection platforms (EPP) and endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools monitor devices for anomalous behavior, leveraging machine‑learning models to flag ransomware encryption patterns or credential‑dumping utilities.",
      "replacement": "Endpoint protection platforms (EPP) and endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools monitor devices for unusual behavior, using machine-learning models to flag ransomware encryption patterns or credential-dumping utilities.",
      "reason": "\"Leveraging\" is a preferred-to-avoid verb; the non-breaking hyphens are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "For data at rest and in transit, robust encryption (AES‑256, TLS 1.3) ensures confidentiality even if attackers bypass other controls.",
      "replacement": "For data at rest and in transit, strong encryption (AES-256, TLS 1.3) keeps data confidential even if attackers bypass other controls.",
      "reason": "\"Robust\" is a discouraged adjective; the non-breaking hyphens are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Identity‑and‑access management (IAM) solutions, especially those employing zero‑trust architectures, verify every user and device before granting access, moving away from the outdated “trusted internal network” paradigm.",
      "replacement": "Identity-and-access management (IAM) solutions, especially those using zero-trust architectures, verify every user and device before granting access, dropping the outdated idea that the internal network can be trusted.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens are banned characters, and curly quotes around \"trusted internal network\" are scare quotes on a normal phrase."
    },
    {
      "original": "Finally, regular patch management—automated wherever possible—closes known vulnerabilities, a simple yet often neglected step that accounts for a large proportion of exploit success (Microsoft, 2022).",
      "replacement": "Regular patch management, automated wherever possible, closes known vulnerabilities, a simple step that is often skipped and that explains a large share of successful exploits (Microsoft, 2022).",
      "reason": "Em dashes are banned characters; \"Finally\" as a list-closing transition was removed along with restructuring."
    },
    {
      "original": "The EDR timeline ends at a truncated process tree…",
      "replacement": "The EDR timeline ends at a truncated process tree...",
      "reason": "The ellipsis glyph (…) is a banned non-keyboard character; three periods replace it."
    },
    {
      "original": "Technical tools cannot succeed in isolation; they require clear policies, governance frameworks, and an organizational culture that prioritizes security.",
      "replacement": "Technical tools cannot succeed alone; they need clear policies, governance frameworks, and a company culture that puts security first.",
      "reason": "\"Prioritizes\" is a soft corporate verb better rendered plainly; no banned characters remain in this edit beyond style."
    },
    {
      "original": "Many enterprises adopt internationally recognized standards such as ISO/IEC 27001 or the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) to structure risk assessment, incident response, and continuous improvement.",
      "replacement": "Many companies adopt well-known standards such as ISO/IEC 27001 or the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) to organize risk assessment, incident response, and ongoing improvement.",
      "reason": "\"Internationally recognized\" and \"continuous improvement\" are stock phrases; plainer wording chosen where accuracy is unchanged."
    },
    {
      "original": "These frameworks promote a systematic process: identify critical assets, protect them with appropriate controls, detect anomalies, respond promptly, and recover to a resilient state.",
      "replacement": "These frameworks lay out a step-by-step process: identify important assets, protect them with suitable controls, detect anomalies, respond quickly, and return to a stable state.",
      "reason": "\"Systematic\" and \"resilient\" were softened to concrete terms; no banned characters present but phrasing tightened."
    },
    {
      "original": "Essential providers now face a significantly tighter incident-reporting clock than most internal SLAs.",
      "replacement": "Essential service providers now face a much shorter incident-reporting window than most internal SLAs allow.",
      "reason": "\"Significantly\" is a discouraged intensifier."
    },
    {
      "original": "Board‑level oversight has become a regulatory expectation; in the European Union, the Network and Information Security (NIS) Directive obliges public and essential service providers to report incidents within 24 hours.",
      "replacement": "Board-level oversight is now a regulatory expectation; in the European Union, the Network and Information Security (NIS) Directive requires public and essential service providers to report incidents within 24 hours.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"Board-level\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "By embedding security metrics into key performance indicators (KPIs) and aligning them with business objectives, organizations transform cybersecurity from a cost center into a strategic asset that protects reputation and competitive advantage.",
      "replacement": "When organizations fold security metrics into key performance indicators (KPIs) and tie them to business goals, cybersecurity stops being just a cost center and becomes something that protects reputation and competitive edge.",
      "reason": "The original starts with \"By [gerund]\", a banned structural pattern."
    },
    {
      "original": "Despite layered defenses, the weakest link in most security chains remains the human element.",
      "replacement": "Even with layered defenses, people remain the weakest point in most security setups.",
      "reason": "\"Human element\" and \"weakest link\" are clichéd framing; reworded plainly without changing meaning."
    },
    {
      "original": "Insider threats—whether malicious, negligent, or coerced—account for an estimated 34 % of data breaches (Verizon, 2024).",
      "replacement": "Insider threats, whether deliberate, careless, or forced, account for an estimated 34% of data breaches (Verizon, 2024).",
      "reason": "Em dashes are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Phishing emails still achieve click‑through rates above 20 % in many industries, despite widespread awareness campaigns.",
      "replacement": "Phishing emails still get click-through rates above 20% in many industries, despite broad awareness campaigns.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"click-through\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "Those click-through rates above 20% show phishing remains significantly more effective than most annual awareness programs assume.",
      "replacement": "Click-through rates above 20% mean phishing works far better than most annual awareness programs expect.",
      "reason": "\"Significantly\" is a discouraged intensifier."
    },
    {
      "original": "Cognitive biases, such as the “availability heuristic,” lead employees to underestimate the likelihood of sophisticated attacks, while fatigue from constant security prompts can foster complacency.",
      "replacement": "Mental shortcuts such as the availability heuristic lead employees to play down how likely sophisticated attacks are, and tiredness from repeated security prompts encourages complacency.",
      "reason": "Curly quotes around \"availability heuristic\" are scare quotes on a normal term; \"foster\" is a preferred-to-avoid verb."
    },
    {
      "original": "Addressing this requires more than periodic training; it demands a security‑first culture where safe behavior is reinforced through gamified learning, real‑time phishing simulations, and clear, accessible reporting channels.",
      "replacement": "Fixing this takes more than occasional training. It calls for a culture that puts security first, where good habits are rewarded through game-style learning, live phishing drills, and simple reporting channels anyone can use.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in \"security-first\" and \"real-time\" are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Psychological research suggests that positive reinforcement—recognizing employees who flag suspicious activity—outperforms punitive measures in sustaining vigilance (Anderson & Lee, 2022).",
      "replacement": "Research in psychology indicates that rewarding employees who report suspicious activity works better than punishment for keeping people alert (Anderson & Lee, 2022).",
      "reason": "Em dashes are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Moreover, strict role‑based access controls reduce the damage potential of compromised credentials, limiting insiders to the minimum privileges required for their duties.",
      "replacement": "Strict role-based access controls also cut the damage a stolen credential can do, giving insiders only the minimum permissions their jobs require.",
      "reason": "\"Moreover\" is a banned transition; the non-breaking hyphen in \"role-based\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "Positive reinforcement for reporters is now a standing practice, full stop.",
      "replacement": "Rewarding employees who report suspicious activity is now standard practice.",
      "reason": "\"Full stop\" as a sentence-ending emphasis tag is filler; trimmed for plain prose."
    },
    {
      "original": "Cybersecurity operates within a complex web of legal and ethical obligations. Data‑protection regulations—GDPR in Europe, CCPA in California, and emerging frameworks in Asia‑Pacific—impose stringent requirements for consent, minimization, and breach notification, with fines that can exceed 4 % of global revenue.",
      "replacement": "Cybersecurity sits inside a tangle of legal and ethical duties. Data-protection rules (GDPR in Europe, CCPA in California, and newer frameworks in Asia-Pacific) set tough requirements for consent, data minimization, and breach notice, with fines that can top 4% of global revenue.",
      "reason": "Em dashes and non-breaking hyphens are banned characters; \"complex web\" is inflated metaphor."
    },
    {
      "original": "Beyond compliance, ethical considerations arise around surveillance, algorithmic bias in threat‑detection AI, and the responsible disclosure of vulnerabilities.",
      "replacement": "Past compliance, ethical questions come up around surveillance, biased algorithms in threat-detection AI, and how vulnerabilities get disclosed responsibly.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"threat-detection\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "The “bug bounty” model, championed by companies like Google and Microsoft, incentivizes ethical hackers to report flaws rather than exploit them, fostering a collaborative security ecosystem.",
      "replacement": "The bug bounty model, pushed by firms such as Google and Microsoft, pays ethical hackers to report flaws instead of exploiting them, which builds cooperation across the security field.",
      "reason": "Curly quotes around \"bug bounty\" are scare quotes on a normal term; \"fostering\" and \"ecosystem\" are preferred-to-avoid words."
    },
    {
      "original": "Conversely, governments grapple with the tension between national security imperatives and individual privacy, as illustrated by debates over encryption backdoors and mandatory data‑retention laws.",
      "replacement": "At the same time, governments struggle to balance national security needs against personal privacy, as seen in arguments over encryption backdoors and required data-retention laws.",
      "reason": "\"Conversely\" as a connector and \"grapple\"/\"imperatives\" were softened; the non-breaking hyphen in \"data-retention\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "A balanced approach—transparent legislation, multi‑stakeholder dialogue, and adherence to international norms—remains essential to prevent a fragmented regulatory landscape that could hinder cross‑border threat intelligence sharing.",
      "replacement": "A workable path forward means open lawmaking, talks among many stakeholders, and following international norms, so that patchwork rules do not block cross-border sharing of threat information.",
      "reason": "Em dashes and non-breaking hyphens are banned characters, and \"regulatory landscape\" uses \"landscape\" in the banned metaphorical sense."
    },
    {
      "original": "When prevention fails, rapid detection and coordinated response become critical.",
      "replacement": "When prevention fails, fast detection and a coordinated response matter most.",
      "reason": "\"Critical\" is a discouraged intensifier-class adjective in this list family; plain wording substituted."
    },
    {
      "original": "Effective incident response (IR) follows a structured lifecycle: preparation, identification, containment, eradication, recovery, and post‑incident analysis.",
      "replacement": "Good incident response (IR) runs through a set sequence: prepare, identify, contain, eradicate, recover, and review after the incident.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"post-incident\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "Central to preparation is an up‑to‑date IR plan, rehearsed through tabletop exercises and live drills that involve IT, legal, communications, and senior management.",
      "replacement": "Preparation hinges on a current IR plan, practiced in tabletop exercises and live drills covering IT, legal, communications, and senior leadership.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in \"up-to-date\" are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Automated security orchestration, automation, and response (SOAR) platforms accelerate containment by executing predefined playbooks—for example, isolating a compromised endpoint within seconds of detecting ransomware encryption.",
      "replacement": "Security orchestration, automation, and response (SOAR) platforms speed up containment by running preset playbooks, such as pulling a hacked endpoint offline seconds after ransomware encryption shows up.",
      "reason": "The em dash before \"for example\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "In the recovery phase, organizations must verify data integrity, restore backed‑up systems, and communicate openly with stakeholders to keep trust.",
      "replacement": "During recovery, organizations must check data integrity, restore backed-up systems, and talk plainly with stakeholders to hold onto trust.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"backed-up\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "Post‑incident lessons learned feed into continuous improvement, ensuring that the next attack encounters a more hardened environment.",
      "replacement": "Lessons from each incident go into steady improvements, so the next attack meets a tougher setup.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"Post-incident\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "Building resilience also involves business continuity planning, diversified supply chains, and cyber‑insurance policies that limit financial damage.",
      "replacement": "Resilience also means business continuity plans, varied supply chains, and cyber-insurance policies that cap financial losses.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"cyber-insurance\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "Artificial intelligence (AI) is a double‑edged sword in cybersecurity.",
      "replacement": "Artificial intelligence (AI) cuts both ways in cybersecurity.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"double-edged\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "On the defensive side, AI‑driven analytics can sift through massive log volumes, identifying subtle anomalies that evade rule‑based systems.",
      "replacement": "On defense, AI-driven analytics sort through huge log volumes and spot small anomalies that rule-based systems miss.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in \"AI-driven\" and \"rule-based\" are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Generative models now assist in automating threat‑intel synthesis and vulnerability prioritization.",
      "replacement": "Generative models now help automate threat-intel summaries and rank vulnerabilities.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"threat-intel\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "However, adversaries exploit the same technology to craft convincing deep‑fake phishing messages, automate credential‑stuffing attacks, and discover novel exploit chains.",
      "replacement": "Attackers use the same technology to write believable deepfake phishing messages, run credential-stuffing attacks at scale, and find new exploit chains.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in \"deep-fake\" and \"credential-stuffing\" are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Quantum computing presents a longer‑term challenge: once large‑scale quantum processors become operational, they could break widely used public‑key algorithms (RSA, ECC).",
      "replacement": "Quantum computing poses a longer-term problem: when large-scale quantum machines work, they may crack common public-key algorithms such as RSA and ECC.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in \"longer-term\", \"large-scale\", and \"public-key\" are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "The cryptographic community is responding with post‑quantum cryptography (PQC) standards currently being finalized by NIST, which aim to replace vulnerable schemes with lattice‑based, hash‑based, or code‑based alternatives.",
      "replacement": "Cryptographers are answering with post-quantum cryptography (PQC) standards that NIST is finishing now, meant to swap weak schemes for lattice-based, hash-based, or code-based ones.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in post-quantum, lattice-based, hash-based, and code-based are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Meanwhile, blockchain technologies offer promising use‑cases for immutable audit trails, decentralized identity management, and secure software‑supply‑chain provenance, though scalability and regulatory acceptance remain hurdles.",
      "replacement": "Blockchain tools show promise for tamper-proof audit logs, distributed identity management, and verifiable software-supply-chain records, though scale and regulatory buy-in are still obstacles.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in \"use-cases\" and \"software-supply-chain\" are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Integrating these emerging tools wisely will be pivotal for maintaining a technological edge against increasingly sophisticated adversaries.",
      "replacement": "Bringing these new tools in carefully will decide whether defenders stay ahead of ever-smarter attackers.",
      "reason": "\"Pivotal\" is a discouraged adjective."
    },
    {
      "original": "Cyber threats disregard geopolitical borders, making international cooperation indispensable.",
      "replacement": "Cyber threats ignore borders, so countries have to work together.",
      "reason": "\"Indispensible-class\" intensifier tone removed; plain wording preferred."
    },
    {
      "original": "Multilateral platforms such as the United Nations’ Group of Governmental Experts (UNGGE) and the NATO Cooperative Cyber Defence Centre of Excellence (CCDCOE) facilitate norm‑setting, information exchange, and joint exercises.",
      "replacement": "Groups such as the United Nations' Group of Governmental Experts (UNGGE) and the NATO Cooperative Cyber Defence Centre of Excellence (CCDCOE) help set norms, share information, and run joint exercises.",
      "reason": "The curly apostrophe in \"Nations’\" is a banned character; \"facilitate\" and the non-breaking hyphen in \"norm-setting\" are avoided words/characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Regional arrangements like the European Union’s Cybersecurity Act and the Asia‑Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Cybersecurity Strategy promote harmonized standards and rapid incident‑sharing mechanisms, such as Computer Emergency Response Teams (CERTs).",
      "replacement": "Regional deals like the European Union's Cybersecurity Act and the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Cybersecurity Strategy push aligned standards and quick incident-sharing setups such as Computer Emergency Response Teams (CERTs).",
      "reason": "Curly apostrophes in \"Union's\" and non-breaking hyphens in \"Asia-Pacific\" and \"incident-sharing\" are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "Public‑private partnerships further bolster collective defense; the Cyber Threat Alliance and the Forum of Incident Response and Security Teams (FIRST) gather threat intelligence from diverse sectors, enabling fast spread of Indicators of Compromise (IoCs).",
      "replacement": "Public-private partnerships add to shared defense; the Cyber Threat Alliance and the Forum of Incident Response and Security Teams (FIRST) pool threat intelligence across sectors, so Indicators of Compromise (IoCs) move quickly.",
      "reason": "\"Furthermore\"-style connector removed and \"bolster\" is a preferred-to-avoid verb; non-breaking hyphen in \"Public-private\" is a banned character."
    },
    {
      "original": "While geopolitical tensions sometimes impede data sharing, the growing recognition that cyber‑attacks threaten global stability has spurred unprecedented collaboration, a trend likely to intensify as states wrestle with attribution challenges and the need for coordinated deterrence.",
      "replacement": "Political friction still blocks some data sharing, but the widening agreement that cyber-attacks endanger global stability has driven close cooperation, a pattern expected to grow as countries face hard attribution questions and the need for joint deterrence.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"cyber-attacks\" is a banned character; \"unprecedented\" was dropped as an intensifier."
    },
    {
      "original": "Looking ahead, cybersecurity will be shaped by three converging forces: hyper‑connectivity, autonomous systems, and an increasingly skilled adversary pool.",
      "replacement": "Three forces will drive cybersecurity forward: dense connectivity, autonomous systems, and a more skilled base of attackers.",
      "reason": "The non-breaking hyphen in \"hyper-connectivity\" is a banned character; \"Looking ahead\" opener trimmed."
    },
    {
      "original": "The expansion of 5G and edge computing will generate billions of new endpoints, each a potential foothold for attackers.",
      "replacement": "Spreading 5G and edge computing will create billions of new endpoints, each one a way in for attackers.",
      "reason": "No banned characters remained here, but \"potential\" hedging on a stated projection was unnecessary."
    },
    {
      "original": "Autonomous vehicles, smart grids, and industrial robots will demand real‑time, AI‑assisted security controls that can react without human intervention.",
      "replacement": "Self-driving cars, smart grids, and factory robots will need real-time, AI-assisted controls that react without a person in the loop.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens in \"real-time\" and \"AI-assisted\" are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "To meet these demands, organizations should adopt the following strategic recommendations:",
      "replacement": "Organizations facing these demands should consider five moves:",
      "reason": "\"Strategic recommendations\" is padded phrasing; kept concise."
    },
    {
      "original": "1. **Zero‑Trust Architecture** – Treat every access request as untrusted, enforcing continuous verification, least‑privilege policies, and micro‑segmentation.",
      "replacement": "1. **Zero-Trust Architecture** - Assume no access request is trustworthy; verify continuously, apply least-privilege rules, and segment workloads finely.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens and the en dash are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "2. **Secure‑by‑Design Development** – Embed security testing (static and dynamic analysis, software‑bill of materials) early in the software development lifecycle, reducing vulnerabilities before deployment.",
      "replacement": "2. **Secure-by-Design Development** - Put security testing (static and dynamic analysis, a software bill of materials) early in development, cutting vulnerabilities before release.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens and the en dash are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "3. **AI‑Enhanced Threat Intelligence** – Leverage federated learning and privacy‑preserving AI to share insights across enterprises without exposing proprietary data.",
      "replacement": "3. **AI-Powered Threat Intelligence** - Use federated learning and privacy-preserving AI so companies can trade findings without handing over private data.",
      "reason": "\"Leverage\" is a preferred-to-avoid verb; non-breaking hyphens and the en dash are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "4. **Post‑Quantum Preparedness** – Begin migrating critical systems to PQC algorithms, prioritizing those that handle high‑value or long‑term sensitive data.",
      "replacement": "4. **Post-Quantum Readiness** - Start moving important systems to PQC algorithms, beginning with ones holding valuable or long-lived sensitive data.",
      "reason": "Non-breaking hyphens and the en dash are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "5. **Resilience Engineering** – Focus on rapid recovery capabilities—immutable backups, diversified cloud regions, and automated failover—to minimize operational disruption.",
      "replacement": "5. **Resilience Engineering** - Build fast-recovery tools: tamper-proof backups, spread-out cloud regions, and automatic failover, to keep outages short.",
      "reason": "Em dashes and the en dash are banned characters."
    },
    {
      "original": "By intertwining technological innovation with robust governance, continuous education, and global cooperation, the cybersecurity community can transform from a reactive posture to one that anticipates and neutralizes threats before they materialize.",
      "replacement": "Weaving new tools together with solid governance, ongoing training, and worldwide cooperation lets the cybersecurity community get ahead of threats instead of chasing them.",
      "reason": "The sentence starts with \"By [gerund]\", a banned structural pattern, and \"robust\" is a discouraged adjective."
    },
    {
